51.

Are You a Raven?


He’s crazy, Layla realized. It was something she had somehow forgotten. Yes, Duke Herhardt is out of his mind.
“Aren’t you going to answer?” he asked, smiling.
He was clutching her hair with a relatively gentle grip, compared to the ferocity with which he had just chased her to the willow tree. She was too frightened and humiliated to notice this, however.
“Layla,” he said in an even quieter voice.
She tried to back away from him, but the tree trunk behind her blocked her way. Despite the speed with which he had just been running, he had already caught his breath. She, on the other hand, was still panting. Each time her chest rose and fell, she could feel the proximity of his large, strong body more vividly, which only added to her embarrassment.

“Why are you…” she said in a trembling voice as she used all her might to try to push his shoulders away. “Why are you doing this to me?” Realizing that her strength wasn’t enough to push him away, she tried to twist out of his grip.
Amused by her futile attempts, Matthias pressed his body firmly against hers. He smiled even more contently as he felt her body freeze with fear.
“I want you to answer me, not ask another question.” He looked straight into her scowling eyes. “Answer me, Layla.”
He slowly caressed her hair. Its soft, smooth texture seemed to soothe the displeasure he had been feeling all week long. It even made her lack of response feel tolerable.
“Do you think it’s ladylike of you to neither write me back nor answer my question?”
“Layla?”
“Layla Llewellyn.” His grip on her hair gradually tightened.
Overcome by an ominous feeling, she finally replied in anger, “I don’t know what you’re talking about!”
“Didn’t you get my note?” “I don’t know.”
“Layla, you’d better think carefully before speaking.” His smile intensified as he noted the uneasiness in her eyes. “If that worthless messenger pigeon couldn’t deliver a single message, what do you think I’ll do with it?”
“Y-you promised!” she shouted. “You promised not to shoot Phoebe!” “Did I?”
“Your Grace!”
“Now I’m the one who doesn’t know what you’re talking about,” he said with a mischievous grin. If she was going to blatantly lie, he saw no reason why he shouldn’t lie back to her.

She briefly glared at him before lowering her quivering eyelids. “I’m sorry…”
“For what?”
“You already know.”
“For ignoring my note, for running away, or for lying?”
She flinched as he pointed out each of her misdeeds. He liked that she was becoming more docile, but he didn’t like that he couldn’t see her eyes.
“That’s enough with the apologies,” he said, raising her chin with his index finger.
He was handling her face like it was a delicate, fragile object. This made her quiver with fear. He didn’t mind this reaction. The satisfaction he felt from their restored eye contact made up for the slight bitterness he was feeling.
“I guess that pen meant nothing to you, since you don’t seem to care that you lost it.”
“No, it means a lot to me! But…”
She trailed off and tried to turn her head away from him, but he gripped her chin to keep her facing him.
“But?” he replied. He didn’t want their conversation to end any more than he wanted her to avoid his gaze.
With her hair and chin firmly in his grasp, she let out a resigned sigh and said, “I really don’t understand you.”
Her calm, emerald-colored eyes reflected his image. They were beautiful eyes. They made him think of an eternal summer.
“Why do you keep stealing my things?” she asked.
He raised his eyebrows in reaction to her accusatory question. He knew that she was the only person in Karlsbar, if not in the entire empire, who

would dare to talk to him like he was a petty thief. He was so flabbergasted he couldn’t help but chuckle.
“Are you… a raven?” she asked, scowling at his jet-black hair and his blue eyes.
“A raven?” he asked with a puzzled expression on his face.
She kept speaking her mind. “You’re always stealing my… that is, you’re the one who’s in the wrong, so I don’t understand why I should be the one to go to you and beg you to give me my things back every time. It’s not fair.”
Her voice was steadily rising as she grew angrier. He was happy to hear it. “Is that why you didn’t reply to my note?” he asked, smirking.
Suddenly she felt foolish, and was at a loss for words. She had gathered all her courage to rebut him, but he had dismissed her with a single derisive question.
Just when she was about to speak again, he cut her off. “Layla.”
Her combativeness had disappeared and been replaced by a strange, hard- to- describe feeling. She quickly lowered her eyes as she struggled to think of what to say.
“Layla,” he said a little more slowly, as if he were humming a tune.
She turned her head to the side and her ear touched against his chest. She heard the faint sound of his heart beating. She worried that he might be able to hear her heart beating as well.
She pressed her pale hands against his shoulders again. She knew it was no use, but she felt she had to try something.
When he finally took a step back to humor her, she let out the breath she had been holding. She was timid but daring, docile but nasty. These contradictions only made her more fascinating to him, and more enticing.

“Please give me back my pen,” she said.
He laughed. She never disappoints. “I don’t have it,” he calmly replied. “I threw it away.
“You WHAT ?”
“I figured you didn’t need it, since you didn’t come looking for it.” “Then why did you come to the cottage…?”
“I felt I needed to tell you, Layla,” he said, shrugging. “I’m a gentleman, after all.” Still clutching her hair, he started to caress it again. “I’m the greatest gentleman in Karlsbar, as even you have acknowledged, Miss Llewellyn.”
He loosened his grip and her hair spilled down onto her shoulders. She took a step back, glaring at him. Before she could take another step, however, she let out a pained yelp.
He had suddenly grabbed the last lock of her hair before it could slip through his fingers.
“Am I not?” he asked, looking with delight at her eyes filling up with tears from the pain.
He’s crazy, she thought again as she struggled to get her hair out of his grip. Grinning, he started playing with the end of the hair he was holding.
I can’t believe this guy is the head of the House of Herhardt! As she started to worry about Arvis’s future, she stumbled backward. He had suddenly let go of her hair. She heard the sound of his laughter as she fell to the ground, and then everything went black.
When she regained consciousness, his face was right in front of her again. Only when she felt his arm wrapped around her waist did she realize that he was holding her in his embrace.
Should I bite him? she thought, feeling like a cornered mouse, when suddenly he let her go.

The slyly indifferent look on his face made her shudder.
She quickly ran to the edge of the stream. He remained where he stood, calmly checking his wristwatch.
“I take back what I said,” she shouted. “You’re not a gentleman. Not at all!” “Suddenly you’ve become a harsh critic,” he said, chuckling.
Her cheeks flushed red. “It’s true.” “Ah, is it?”
“Yes! If you’re a gentleman, I’m a queen!” She hated herself for giving him such servile praise before.
He raised his eyebrows and smirked as he fixed his coat’s lapel. Then he bowed deeply to her, as if he were a servant bowing to a queen, before turning and starting to walk away. The exquisite elegance of the gesture filled her with embarrassment.
She stared blankly at his back as he receded into the distance. He had chased her down like a wild beast, tormented her, and then humiliated her all the more by bidding her this graceful farewell.







The car was waiting for him when Matthias arrived at the cabin. He got straight in. The irritation and displeasure he had been feeling when he had left the cabin were completely gone now.
The car gathered speed as it drove down the road next to the river. He gazed out the window at the Schulter River for a moment. The slight smile

lingering on his face disappeared when turned to look at the documents and letters his assistant had brought for him.
To cheer himself up again, he reached into his coat pocket and retrieved the pen.
Layla Llewellyn—the gold lettering glittered in the afternoon sun.
He slowly ran his fingers over the pen’s glossy surface. He removed the cap, smiling. The sound of the pen scratching against paper filled the sunny interior of the car.







After delivering the rest of the mail to the school, the mailman held out a small package towards Layla. “This is for you, Miss Llewellyn.”
“For me?” she asked, taking the package. It was strange to be receiving a package at school, rather than at home, and she didn’t recognize the sender’s name or address. “Are you sure it’s not a mistake?”
“Pardon?” He looked closely at the address label. “Hmm… No, it’s definitely right.”
She smiled and shook her head. Despite the unfamiliar sender and address, her name was clearly written on the package.
“Thank you,” she said before hurrying into the school.
It was the newest teacher’s duty to receive and distribute the mail each day. She always started at the principal’s office and went from there to each classroom. By the time she was done distributing all the mail, her break was almost over.

She returned to her classroom, sat at her desk, and unwrapped the package she had received. There was a long, narrow box inside, without a note or any other marking.
She checked the sender’s name and address again. It was an unfamiliar name, and an unfamiliar city.
She cocked her head and opened the box, then looked with wonder at the object inside. It was a black pen with gold trim.
The moment she saw it, everything made sense. She knew who had sent it, and for what reason. Her unpleasant memories from the previous weekend came flooding back, and she sighed. The pen looked far more expensive than her original one.
She carefully lifted it out of the box. Her named was engraved on the cap, just like it had been on the pen the duke had thrown away.
The bell indicating the start of classes rang, and the children swarmed into the classroom.
She put the pen back in its box and placed it deep in a drawer of her desk. She hoped that her memories from the weekend would stay there with it, although she knew it was a vain hope.
She stepped in front of her podium and smiled brightly. “Hello, children.
Let’s get started!”




52.

Over and Done With.






It was late afternoon when Marquess Riette von Lindman arrived at the Herhardt mansion. His carriage was followed by another carriage packed
with luggage for his stay at Arvis.
“Welcome, Riette,” Elise von Herhardt said, smiling brightly. “I wish my sister could have come along with you.”
“Ah, yes,” he replied. “Mother is about to leave for a trip.” “Is she going south for the winter again?”
“Yes, she can’t stand the cold,” he replied in a gentle tone. “Where is Dowager Herhardt?”
“You can greet her later. She’s taking a nap right now.”
Elise led Riette to the drawing room. As Matthias’s cousin and friend, Riette had visited Arvis countless times over the years. The Herhardts had always treated him like part of the family.

After having tea with his aunt, he went out for a leisurely stroll in the forest. Matthias wasn’t going to be home until night, and it was going to be a while until the servants had finished unloading his luggage and preparing his room. More than anything, he was curious about the girl who lived by the forest, the girl who was jeopardizing Matthias and Claudine’s perfect life- Layla.
Of course, Riette had long been aware of Layla, but had never paid her much mind. He had just lumped her in with the rest of Arvis’s staff. He had pitied her for her disadvantaged background, and he had known that she was quite pretty. That was all. He was thus quite surprised when he found out that she was the woman that Matthias had taken an interest in.
Scandals involving affairs between aristocrats and their maids were common and usually attracted little attention. It would be a different story, however, if the aristocrat in question was Duke Herhardt, even if it proved to be nothing more than a passing fancy.
“Duke Herhardt lied. ”
As the gardener’s cottage appeared in the distance, something popped into Riette’s mind that Claudine had told him one evening late the previous summer.
“He lied in order to separate Layla and the doctor’s son. ”
After saying this, Claudine had slowly turned towards Riette and smiled.
Or rather, her lips had smiled; her eyes had remained cold and emotionless. “Matthias von Herhardt lied and schemed so that he could have that lowly
orphan for himself. Can you believe it, Riette?” she had asked, giggling.
Riette had asked her what sort of lies Matthias had told, but she hadn’t elaborated.

“I hope he gets her as soon as possible, ” she had said, calmly gazing out the window at the sun sinking below the horizon after a long summer day at the Brandt estate. “The sooner he has her, the sooner he’ll be done with her, ’’she had quietly added, her face resembling that of a bored spectator at a third-rate stage performance.
She had never shown the slightest hint of jealousy towards Layla. She had displayed a slightly derisive attitude towards her noble fiance’s taste, as if she were disappointed he hadn’t set his sights a bit higher, but that was it. And Riette knew that this was how she really felt.
If Claudine had instead seemed jealous or unhappy about the situation, Riette might have had the courage to tell her not to get stuck in a marriage that was going to make her unhappy when she could marry someone else who would make her happy —Riette himself. But she had continued to see Matthias as the key to her future happiness. And Riette was well aware of this as well.
That day, the two of them had enjoyed light conversation over tea. Dinner had been similarly pleasant and carefree. The next morning, Riette had left the Brandt estate. Claudine had remained standing in the doorway until his car had drifted out of sight.
As he reflected on this memory, Riette wondered why it had suddenly arisen in his mind. He let out a hollow laugh.
Just then, he arrived at the cottage. He noticed that the cottage was linked to the unfenced hunting ground by the forest path.
The sun had already begun to set, but the inside of the cottage was dark. Reluctant to turn back around right away after walking all that way for nothing, he decided to wait for a bit. He leaned on the porch’s railing and looked up at the trees, which had dropped about half their leaves.

Riette loved a woman who was never going to let love make her unhappy.
Perhaps this was the reason for his own unhappiness.
He accepted this fate. He loved his simple, carefree life, and he wasn’t above carrying out an even more simple, carefree bit of mischief, especially if it would bring him entertainment and bring Claudine happiness.
Time drew on. Just as he was lighting a cigarette, he heard the sound of footsteps approaching on the forest path. Soon he could also hear the sound of two people talking.
Gazing in the direction of the sounds, he took a long drag of his cigarette and slowly exhaled the smoke. Before long, he saw a man as big as a bear emerge from the forest next to a woman scarcely half his size. They stopped at the edge of the cottage’s front yard when they saw him.
He smiled and started walking towards them. The gardener gave him a terse greeting once he recognized him, and the woman bowed her head.
“Hello, you must be the bird girl who lives by the forest,” Riette said with the same calm attitude he had shown when he had encountered her crying in the forest on her way to bury the birds Matthias had killed. “Ah, I suppose you’re not a bird girl anymore. Now you’re a bird lady. Or would you prefer I call you a bird teacher?”
There was a doubtful look in her bespectacled eyes. She frowned as she listened to him prattle on.
His impression of her remained unchanged. She was pretty, but she wasn’t a stunning beauty capable of capturing every man’s heart. Nonetheless, he could vaguely sense that there was something special about her, something that had bewitched both his cousin and the doctor’s son.
“Well anyway, nice to meet you, Layla.”







“Be careful of Marquess Lindman,” Bill said in a serious tone as he sat at the dinner table.
Layla chuckled as she set a pot of freshly cooked stew down in the middle of the table.
“Come now, this isn’t a laughing matter,” he said. “Uncle Bill…”
“Do you think that idler really came all the way over here on a leisurely stroll? No way. He was definitely up to something.” He tore his slice of bread in half roughly. “He went right up to you and started blathering on about this and that. I’m just saying, it was suspicious. Be careful.”
“Yes, Uncle Bill,” she replied, knowing that agreeing was the only way to get him to stop harping on this issue.
He frowned, sensing that she was just humoring him. “You should take my words to heart, Layla. Not all aristocrats are as decent and respectable as Duke Herhardt.”
“Yes, I kn… huh?” She furrowed her brow. She wanted to dispute his characterization of the duke, but she couldn’t. He had described Duke Herhardt the way everyone knew him, besides her.
She nodded reluctantly, keeping her feelings to herself, and took a quick bite of her bread. As she chewed, her memories of that indecent, unrespectable man only intensified. As she thought of the mocking way he had bowed to her,

and the excessively beautiful pen he had sent to the school, the bread went down the wrong pipe and she started coughing violently.
“What’s the matter?”
“Nothing,” she said, shaking her head. “I was just so hungry I ate too fast.” She took off her glasses and wiped away the tears that the coughing had caused.
Bill stared at her for a moment, then burst into laughter. “At times like these, you still seem like a child,” he gently chided. “Eat a lot, but take your time.” He took a sizable hunk of meat from the pot and placed it in her bowl. Then another. Soon there was a pile of meat rising from her bowl.
“That’s too much!” she shouted.
He was stubborn as always. “Come now, Layla, I like kids who can eat like a cow.”
“I’m not a kid anymore,” she protested.
In response, he placed one last piece of meat on top of the pile.







They spent the rest of their dinner laughing and chatting. Layla wasn’t able to eat as much as a cow, but she managed to eat enough to make Bill happy.
After the meal, he fixed her creaky desk chair while she cleaned up the kitchen. Then she poured two large mugs of coffee and they sat on the porch, gazing at the leaves falling in the forest. The weather had already started to get cold, but they still finished each day chatting on the porch, as they did every year until winter rolled around.

After that, they got ready for bed. Pausing before he went into his room, he said, “Sleep well, Layla.”
“You too, Uncle Bill,” she replied. A sliver of light from outside shone through a gap in the curtain, illuminating the gentle smile on her face. “Good night.”
She went to her room, sat in the chair he had just fixed for her, and graded her students’ quizzes. She then read a few chapters of a mystery novel she had borrowed from the library and wrote replies to letters from some fellow teachers she had made friends with.
As she finished the last reply and capped the old, worn-out pen she was using, she was reminded of the new pen the duke had sent her.
She stared into space for a moment, then resolutely opened the desk’s drawer. She had reluctantly brought the pen home. There it was, still in its box.
She thought of the dismissive things people had always used to say about her when she had made small mistakes as a child.
“I knew it.”
“What can you expect from a child raised without parents?”
These comments had always been said with a mixture of pity and contempt.
At first, she had been perplexed by the fact that she was reprimanded more than her peers for making the same mistakes as they did, or even when she was much more well-behaved than they were. But as she grew up, she came to understand that life wasn’t fair. There were double standards wherever one looked.
This made her want to be even better. Even if she could never be perfect, she wanted at least to live a life that others didn’t pity her for. Her resolve was

strengthened each time she faced another cruel double standard. She felt she owed this to Uncle Bill, who had so lovingly raised her.
With these thoughts in mind, she took the box with the pen in it out of the drawer and grabbed a piece of brown paper.
After meticulously wrapping the box in the paper, she carefully wrote an address on it, using the same old, worn-out pen she had written the letters with. The recipient was the lord of Arvis, Duke Matthias von Herhardt. For the return address, she wrote down the same unfamiliar name and address that had been on the original package she had received at the school.
The next morning, at the crack of dawn, she put the box in her bag and set off for work. She had to leave earlier than usual so that she could stop by the neighboring village’s post office on her way.
After returning the gift, she felt like a weight had been lifted off her chest.
She believed that it was all over and done with now. She was sure of it.




53.

That Bird’s Owner.






“This pains my heart every time I see it, Matthias,” Riette said, clicking his tongue as he opened Matthias’s liquor cabinet.
Matthias wasn’t much of a drinker, but the liquor cabinet in his room was always fully stocked with the finest spirits.
“It’s not right that you should neglect these precious bottles,” Riette said with a bold grin as he took a bottle to the table.
As always, Matthias seemed not to care what his cousin was doing.
The music coming from the phonograph blended pleasingly with the crackling of the logs in the fireplace. Riette filled a liquor glass that a servant had brought and held it out to Matthias. The graceful motion with which Matthias accepted the glass resembled the piano melody flowing through the room.

Riette leaned so far back into the sofa that he was practically lying down and looked closely at Matthias as if he were observing a stranger. Matthias was lost in thought, staring at the rim of his glass. The little canary, which now felt like it had become part of the room, was cheerfully hopping about next to him.
As Riette continued to look at Matthias, he recalled something someone had said about him at a social club: “Your cousin is a gentlemanly demon.”
Matthias had never spoken to him about his time serving as a captain on the overseas war front. Rather than being from humbleness, this seemed to be more because he attached no significance to his service. It was instead from the mouths of other aristocratic officers that had served alongside him that word of his heroic exploits had spread.
Riette hadn’t served in that war, but from their descriptions, he felt like he had seen Matthias on the battlefield with his own eyes. He had a peculiar attitude—unlike other young soldiers, he was neither a passionate fighter intoxicated by the thrill of battle nor was he utterly bored by the tedium of military duties. He systematically carried out the tasks assigned to him, and though he didn’t deny the satisfaction of accomplishment, he didn’t seem to think any of it meant much, either. Anyone talking about Captain Herhardt inevitably concluded their account by saying, “So… I really don’t understand him.”
Riette knew better than anyone that this dispirited comment summed up Matthias von Herhardt better than any other description.
“I don’t understand him, ’’always uttered with a sigh.
Riette sighed as he thought about it now. I don’t understand him.
A lifetime of observing Matthias had brought him to that same conclusion. Just as combining all the beautiful colors of the rainbow produced pure white

light, so all of Matthias’s various qualities combined to produce an inscrutable whole.
He was a noble aristocrat, a worthy successor, a good cousin, and an honorable soldier. Each of these qualities was clear and distinct in itself, yet the man to which they added up was impossible to parse.
Some praised Duke Herhardt for the way he suppressed his personal desires so that he could fulfill his duties, but Riette was skeptical of this. The Matthias he knew had no desires to suppress. And this made his attitude towards this Layla Llewellyn girl all the more perplexing.
Riette smiled, his lips wet with the drink. Matthias looked at him. The canary was now sitting on his shoulder. One might have thought this would bother Matthias, but he didn’t seem to mind it at all.
“How long do you think you’ll continue to act as that bird’s owner?” Riette asked.
“As long as I want to,” Matthias replied without hesitation. He then finally took a sip of the liquor glass he had been holding. The bird flew up into the air briefly before landing back on his shoulder and rubbing its beak on the strap of his suspenders.
“How long do you think that will be?” Riette asked.
“Who knows.” Matthias set down his glass and leaned against the armrest of his seat. His onyx cufflinks reflected the light from the fireplace.
“Aren’t you curious about anything?” Riette asked. “Why I’m here at Arvis, what I’m planning to do here, anything like that?”
“Nope,” Matthias indifferently replied. Riette had appeared at Arvis out of the blue and stayed for weeks before. It was nothing out of the ordinary.
“Fine, be that way,” Riette replied, laughing loudly before he drained his glass. He looked closely at Matthias and thought, Is it lust? That seemed like the

most likely explanation. It’s just a man s lust for a pretty woman. It’s nature. Even someone without any emotions would still have natural instincts. But why that orphan, of all people? He’s always been immune to the charms of women much more beautiful and elegant than her. Soon he felt like his thoughts were going in circles, so he decided to stop pondering the matter.
The bird was hopping about on the table now. Matthias gave a short whistle and it slew right back to him.
After witnessing this, Riette tried calling the bird himself, whistling an elaborate melody. Perched on the back of Matthias’s hand, the bird cocked its head but showed no inclination to fly towards Riette.
“What the heck,” Riette said, chuckling incredulously. “Can a bird recognize its owner?”
Suddenly Riette recalled the wary look Layla had given him. He didn’t expect her unfriendly facade to last. If she had opened up to the doctor’s son and the duke, why wouldn’t she open up to him as well? He was also sure that Matthias would eventually abandon Layla for the sake of Claudine. This was Claudine’s desired outcome.
“Want to go hunting this weekend?” Riette asked as he poured himself another glass.
Matthias thought for a moment then gave an unexpected response. “You go alone. I’ll make sure everything is ready for you.”
Riette was taken aback. “What?” As far back as he could remember, Matthias had never once turned down a chance to go hunting. “Are you serious?”
Before Matthias could answer, there was a knock at the door. It was Hessen, the butler. “The mail has arrived, Your Grace,” he said, walking towards Matthias with a tray with several envelopes and a package on it.

Riette stared aloofly at the butler. Why does he have to come in here right now for something as trivial as that? After a moment, he realized that the butler was giving him a look that seemed to suggest he needed to leave Matthias’s bedroom.
“I’ll see you tomorrow, then, Duke Herhardt,” he said, waving. He then took his glass and left the room.
Hessen waited until Riette’s footsteps were no longer audible, then said, “There’s a package, Your Grace.”
“A package?” Matthias replied, accepting the small box Hessen was stiffly holding out to him. He didn’t recognize the sender’s name or address. “Who’s this?”
“A relative of mine,” Hessen replied. “I used this name and address when I was carrying out your recent command.”
“My recent command…? Ah.” He realized after a moment that Hessen was referring to his command from the previous week to send a good pen to Layla. Once he realized this, everything suddenly made sense.
“Um, Your Grace, may I-”
“All right,” Matthias said, cutting Hessen off as he often did. “You can go.”
Hessen glanced at his master’s fingers tapping against the box, then left at once.
Once the door was closed, Matthias stood up, holding the box in his hand.
He tore off the wrapping paper and threw it in the fireplace.
He opened the box and found a pen and a note sitting neatly inside. He lifted the note and carefully read it.
After thinking about it, I realized that losing the pen was my fault. It was my fault that I fell down, that I didn I pick up everything I had dropped, and that I didn’t get back my possession at the proper time. There’s no need for Your Grace to take

responsibility for my mistake. lam thus returning something I should never have received in the first place.
The note was unsigned, but it was clear enough from the self-assured attitude in the message that it had come from Layla. As Matthias read it a few more times, he raised his eyebrows and started to smile, eventually letting out a dry laugh.
The rolling fire devoured the crumpled note in an instant. It then made short work of the box and the shiny new pen.
Gazing into the flames, Matthias’s face showed a self-mocking sneer, then anger, then humiliation, and finally amusement. These distinct emotions melded into one, then disappeared from his face, leaving in their place a calm expression overlaid by the glowing of the fire.







Classes were ending early today, and yet Layla was more busy than usual because it was the day of the school council meeting. The school’s building was quite old and rather small, so they were going to discuss the possibility of expanding it.
Layla had been assigned the task of setting up the meeting room. She arranged enough desks and chairs for all the members who would be in attendance and prepared writing implements and notepads as well.
Mrs. Graeber approached with hurried steps. “Miss Llewellyn, are you finished?”

Layla looked around the meeting room one last time, then smiled and nodded her head. “Yep, it’s ready.”
“Then let’s get out of here. The sponsors are arriving.”
“Already?”  Layla quickly straightened her dress, then followed Mrs.
Graeber out of the room.
There was a long line of the sponsors’ lavish carriages and automobiles entering through the school’s front gate.
Layla felt an ominous feeling for a moment, then shook her head to rid herself of it. She had read the list of sponsors who would be at today’s meeting several times, and there was no one from the Herhardt family. And yet an uneasy feeling lingered in her chest.
She figured that this was probably because of the gift she had returned. It had been several days, but the duke hadn’t responded in any manner. He had neither written back to her nor had he paid her a visit and questioned her as he had in the past. She had been on edge for days out of fear that he was going to subject her to more humiliation. Perhaps she had injured the duke’s pride. Well, it had to happen eventually, she thought.
Despite how unknowledgeable she was about romance, she was old enough to have a vague sense of the duke’s lustfulness and to know that that could be a problem for her.
She hated it all—the duke, his lust, and the impact his lust could have on her. She was tired of being swept up in a world she had nothing to do with. She was tired of getting hurt. She had tried to express this in the note she included with the pen, so perhaps the duke understood now. Perhaps that aloof man’s silence indicated that he was submitting to her wishes.
Gradually calming down, she took her spot at the end of the line of school employees greeting the sponsors. It was a cold, rainy autumn day. There had

been concern that the weather might have lowered the attendance, but fortunately, this was not the case—everyone on the attendee list showed up.
