“And so, until you get married, while we can stilt be together here at Arvis, I want us to be lovers’
He wasn’t sure what the difference between a mistress and a lover was, but it seemed that to Layla, they were as distinct as night and day.
“Just once, before I’m hidden in the shadows forever, I want to have the experience of being lovers with someone.”
He vividly recalled the way she had looked as she whispered that with tears in her eyes.
Shadows. There was no word that fit that woman less. She seemed like she was made out of the brightest, most beautiful rays of sunlight. He had been charmed by that light. It had blinded him. Wanting to live in that light, he had seized it for himself. He couldn’t let it go.
But now he was realizing that this longing of his meant that she would have to live in the shadows. The realization came as a shock to him. If you’re in the shadows forever, will you still shine like you do now? This unpleasant question

had popped into his mind, causing him to suddenly embrace Layla tightly. It was as if he thought that holding her close to himself would keep her light shining forever.
His reverie was interrupted by the sound of his mother clearing her throat. “The points you’ve made are true, Matthias, but I think it’s too hasty of a decision. If you send Bill Remmer to Ratse, what will become of his adopted daughter?”
“She will move to Ratse with Mr. Remmer. And our family is going to pay for her college tuition, Mother.”
“You’re sending her to college? Our family is sponsoring the gardener’s adopted daughter?”
“Yes As I recall, Grandmother already expressed her intention to do so last year”
He looked to his grandmother for confirmation. She was unable to deny it. “Yes. I did say that.”
Having previously offered to pay for Layla’s college tuition as a gift, Dowager Herhardt had reiterated her willingness to become Layla’s sponsor after her tuition money was stolen.
A slight smile appeared on Matthias’s lips. “I think it can thus be said that both of your intentions will be met if we have Bill Remmer transfer to the Ratse mansion, and we sponsor his adopted daughter’s college education.”
The two women exchanged glances. Neither of them could find any way to refute Matthias’s point.
“Then I will proceed with carrying out both of your wills.”
He bowed respectfully and left the drawing room. He had a busy afternoon schedule and had to depart at once. He could easily guess what his mother and grandmother would say to each other after he left. However, he didn’t care.

He calmly walked down the hallway, thinking of the word shadows. The more he thought about it, the less it seemed to suit Layla.




